\label{prop:paired_features}
Under the constraints in Eq.~\eqref{eq:featureconstraints}, each feature in Eq.~\eqref{eq:features} is objective and convex in the independent variables $(\bm F,J)$ on $J>0$. Its value and engineering-strain gradient at the identity, evaluated with $J=\det\bm F$, are
\begin{equation}
 z_i^0=\frac1{p_i}+\frac1{q_i},\qquad
 \partial_{\bm e}z_i\big|_{\bm0}=\rho_i(1,1,0)^T,
 \qquad \rho_i=2-\frac{b_i}{p_i}-\frac{c_i}{q_i}.
 \label{eq:referencefeatures}
\end{equation}

\emph{Convexity and objectivity.}
For any fixed learned parameter set, the directions $\bm d_i$ and $\bm d_i^\perp$ are independent of deformation, so the maps $\bm F\mapsto\bm F\bm d_i$ and $\bm F\mapsto\bm F\bm d_i^\perp$ are linear. Composing the convex building block above with these maps, multiplying by the positive factors $1/p_i$ and $1/q_i$, and adding the two contributions proves convexity of $z_i$ in independent $(\bm F,J)$. A left proper rotation changes $\bm F\bm d$ to $\bm Q\bm F\bm d$ without changing its norm, so each contribution is objective.

\emph{Reference value and derivative.}
The physical relation $J=\det\bm F$ is now restored, with every learned parameter still fixed. At $\bm E=\bm0$, both directional invariants and $J$ equal one, so the two contributions give $z_i^0=1/p_i+1/q_i$. Moreover, $I_4(\bm d_i)=\bm d_i^T(\bm I+2\bm E)\bm d_i$ and $J^2=(1+2E_{11})(1+2E_{22})-4E_{12}^2$ imply $\partial_{\bm E}I_4(\bm d_i)=2\bm d_i\otimes\bm d_i$ and $\partial_{\bm E}J|_0=\bm I$. Applying the product rule to the first contribution gives
\begin{equation}
 \left.\partial_{\bm E}\frac{I_4(\bm d_i)^{p_i}}{p_iJ^{b_i}}\right|_0
 =\frac1{p_i}\bigl[2p_i\bm d_i\otimes\bm d_i-b_i\bm I\bigr]
 =2\bm d_i\otimes\bm d_i-\frac{b_i}{p_i}\bm I.
\end{equation}
The perpendicular contribution has the same form with $(\bm d_i,p_i,b_i)$ replaced by $(\bm d_i^\perp,q_i,c_i)$. Since $\bm d_i\otimes\bm d_i+\bm d_i^\perp\otimes\bm d_i^\perp=\bm I$, adding the two contributions removes the directional dependence at the reference and gives
\begin{equation}
 \left.\partial_{\bm E}z_i\right|_0
 =2(\bm d_i\otimes\bm d_i+\bm d_i^\perp\otimes\bm d_i^\perp)
 -\left(\frac{b_i}{p_i}+\frac{c_i}{q_i}\right)\bm I
 =\rho_i\bm I.
\end{equation}
In the work-conjugate engineering-strain convention of Eq.~\eqref{eq:voigt}, this tensor gradient corresponds to $\partial_{\bm e}z_i|_0=\rho_i(1,1,0)^T$, proving Eq.~\eqref{eq:referencefeatures}.

\emph{Potential structure, objectivity, and stress symmetry (C1)--(C3).}
Every directional norm, $J$, and $\operatorname{tr}\bm C$ are unchanged by left proper rotations, since all of them can be expressed through $\bm d_i^T\bm C\bm d_i$, $\operatorname{tr}\bm C$, and $\sqrt{\det\bm C}$. Hence the complete energy is objective, and differentiation with respect to symmetric strain gives a symmetric material stress. Differentiating this scalar energy supplies (C1) and the major symmetry of its constitutive tangent. Smoothness holds on $\mathrm{GL}^{+}(2)$ because $J>0$, nonzero material directions have nonzero deformed length, and both cores are $C^2$. Because this argument uses left rotations only, it does not impose a material symmetry group.

\emph{Reference normalization (C4).}
Equation~\eqref{eq:energy_reference_value} shows that $W_{\rm learn}$ sets the reference energy, and Eq.~\eqref{eq:energy_reference_stress} shows that $W_{\rm corr}$ cancels the learned reference stress. The calculations for $W_{\rm vol}$ and $W_{\rm dist}$ in Section~\ref{sec:energy_admissibility} show that both growth terms have zero value and zero strain gradient at the reference, so the complete energy satisfies both reference conditions.

\emph{Polyconvexity (C5).} Let $\bm G\in\mathbb R^{2\times2}$ and $j>0$ be independent. Define $\mathcal P(\bm G,j)$ by the right-hand side of Eq.~\eqref{eq:energy}, replacing $\bm z$ by $\bm z(\bm G,j)$, $J$ by $j$, and $\operatorname{tr}\bm C$ by $\|\bm G\|_F^2$. For two such pairs $\bm X_1=(\bm G_1,j_1)$, $\bm X_2=(\bm G_2,j_2)$ and $0\leq t\leq1$,
\begin{align}
 H\!\left(\bm z(t\bm X_1+(1-t)\bm X_2)\right)
 &\leq H\!\left(t\bm z(\bm X_1)+(1-t)\bm z(\bm X_2)\right)\nonumber\\
 &\leq tH(\bm z(\bm X_1))+(1-t)H(\bm z(\bm X_2)).
 \label{eq:convex_composition_proof}
\end{align}
The first inequality uses feature convexity and core monotonicity, and the second uses core convexity, so $W_{\rm learn}$ is convex up to its constant reference subtraction. The correction $W_{\rm corr}=-r(j-1)$ is affine for either sign of $r$, while $W_{\rm vol}$ and $W_{\rm dist}$ are convex in independent $(\bm G,j)$ because their nonaffine parts are positive multiples of $-\log j$, $(j-1)^2$, and $\|\bm G\|_F^2$. Consequently, $\mathcal P$ is convex and $W(\bm F)=\mathcal P(\bm F,\det\bm F)$, establishing Eq.~\eqref{eq:polyconvex_definition}.

\emph{Growth (C6).}
Convexity of $H$ gives its supporting-plane inequality at $\bm z^0$,
\begin{equation}
 H(\bm z)\geq H(\bm z^0)+\sum_i h_i(z_i-z_i^0)
 \geq H(\bm z^0)-\sum_i h_i z_i^0.
 \label{eq:supporting_plane}
\end{equation}
The second inequality uses $h_i,z_i\geq0$. Hence $W_{\rm learn}$ has a deformation-independent lower bound and cannot cancel the analytic growth terms. As $J\to0^+$, the logarithmic part of $W_{\rm vol}+W_{\rm dist}$ diverges while $W_{\rm corr}$ remains bounded. As $J\to\infty$, the positive quadratic part of $W_{\rm vol}$ dominates every potentially negative linear or logarithmic term. Finally, if $J$ remains in a compact subset of $(0,\infty)$ while $\|\bm F\|_F\to\infty$, the term $\varepsilon\|\bm F\|_F^2/2$ in $W_{\rm dist}$ diverges and all determinant-only terms remain bounded. These are the three stated growth limits.
